# The chart, opened over a node instead of over the Status cards.
#
# chart.scene shows the component doing the thing it was built for: two lines about the radio in
# your hand, on one domain, with the thresholds ruled across them. This is the same component
# spent a second time, and the scene is arranged around the one decision that made it possible -
# a chart carries *one* vertical, so a temperature in degrees and a humidity in percent are two
# screens rather than two lines. Drawn together, only one of them would have been measured
# against the axis the reader is reading.
#
# What it costs is a series in history.c and a level of the Nodes tab. What it buys is the
# question a solar repeater in a field is actually opened for: not "how warm is it" - the row
# already says that - but "has this box been cooking all afternoon".

scene demo
scale 4
delay 220

# ---- an afternoon of readings ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Echo Repeater is the demo's solar node, and `environment` is one EnvironmentMetrics report: a
# temperature and a humidity arriving together, which is how the wire carries them. Its own verb
# rather than an argument to `battery`, because the two are separate Telemetry variants on
# separate schedules - the store keys each on its own struct having changed, so a node reporting
# air while its battery holds still is the ordinary case and not an edge one.
#
# The readings climb through the afternoon and the humidity falls as they do, which is what air
# does and also what makes the pair worth two screens: they move in opposite directions, so a
# single auto-scaled plot of both would have drawn two lines crossing and meant nothing by it.
tab nodes
environment ECHO 9 78
hold 260
environment ECHO 14 71
hold 260
environment ECHO 21 63
hold 260
environment ECHO 28 55
hold 260
environment ECHO 36 47
hold 260
environment ECHO 44 41
hold 260
environment ECHO 51 36
hold 260

# ---- the row, and then the picture ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Row 6 of the Nodes list is Echo Repeater - row 0 is the Map, so a node's row is its index in
# the roster plus one. Row 21 of its detail is Temperature: the Environment heading is row 20,
# and this node has no device-metrics group above it because a repeater on mains reports no
# battery.
#
# The row is a meter now rather than a bare figure, which is what earns it the press: it has a
# bar, a band and six cells of line in its trailing slot, and A opens the same reading with the
# room to say what its axes are. That is the rule the Status card's verb set - a row carries a
# glance, a screen carries a study - reached from the other direction.
key down 6
key a
key down 21
hold 1400
key a
hold 2600

# ---- and the other one ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# B lands back on the row it was opened from rather than on the list, because the chart is a
# level of the detail. Then one row down and the same press, and the humidity is its own place: a
# slide rather than a repaint, which is the route carrying the *reading* in its slot rather than
# a bare "a chart is open".
#
# The two screens are the argument for the split. Both say "%" or "C" at the ends of their
# vertical and mean it; a shared plot could have labelled only one of them.
key b
key down 1
key a
hold 2600
key b
hold 700
